cff-version: 1.2.0
title: Portable ECG Educational Platform
message: "If you use this project in research or teaching, please cite it using the information below."
type: software

authors:
  - Austin Wong
  - Marly Roufaeil
  - John Gunay
  - Joe Borovoy
  - Ali Testa
  - Jaden Vanderpol

repository-code: https://github.com/YoukaiKouhai/portable-ecg-education

abstract: >
  Students commonly learn about cardiac physiology through static diagrams rather than 
  by observing real physiological signals. Although clinical electrocardiogram (ECG) 
  systems can measure the heart’s electrical activity, these devices are typically 
  expensive, complex, and designed for medical environments rather than educational 
  settings. This project addresses the need for a safe, portable, and affordable ECG 
  demonstration device that enables students to interact with real time cardiac signals 
  in classroom and outreach environments. The proposed system is a self contained handheld 
  ECG demonstration tool built around the AD8232 analog front-end and an Arduino microcontroller 
  with an integrated display. Surface electrodes capture the heart’s bioelectric signals, which 
  are amplified and filtered before being processed and visualized as ECG waveforms. To improve 
  signal clarity in noisy educational environments, the design incorporated both adaptive Kalman 
  filtering for signal smoothing and Butterworth filtering for real time interference reduction, 
  particularly from patient movement. Additionally, the device operates entirely on battery power 
  and includes a built in self test mode to ensure reliable operation. Processed signals are 
  exported for analysis and visualization using both Python and MatLab based tools. By combining 
  safe hardware design with adaptive signal processing and clear waveform visualization, this 
  project aims to create a portable educational electrocardiogram system that enhances student 
  understanding of cardiac electrophysiology and biomedical instrumentation. 

keywords:
  - ECG
  - biomedical engineering
  - signal processing
  - electrocardiography
  - educational tools
  - Arduino
  - MATLAB
  - Python

